[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Queues: PriorityQueue and ArrayDeque in Core Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Java, a Queue is an interface that extends the Collection interface. It represents a collection of elements designed for holding elements prior to processing. Queues typically, but do not necessarily, order elements in a FIFO (first-in-first-out) manner. The `java.util` package provides several implementations of the Queue interface, with `PriorityQueue` and `ArrayDeque` being two of the most commonly used. This tutorial provides a comprehensive overview of these two implementations."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"PriorityQueue"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `PriorityQueue` class implements the Queue interface. Unlike a typical FIFO queue, `PriorityQueue` orders its elements according to their natural ordering or based on a `Comparator` provided at construction time. This means the element with the highest priority (smallest value if using natural ordering, or as defined by the Comparator) will be at the head of the queue."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Characteristics of PriorityQueue"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Elements are ordered based on priority (natural ordering or a Comparator)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Does not allow null elements."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Not thread-safe; use `PriorityBlockingQueue` for concurrent environments."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Provides O(log(n)) time complexity for `add` and `remove` operations (due to heap structure)."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a PriorityQueue"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can create a `PriorityQueue` in several ways:"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Using natural ordering (elements must implement `Comparable`):"},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Using a custom `Comparator`:"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.reverseOrder());"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Operations with PriorityQueue"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here are some common operations performed on a `PriorityQueue`:"},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.PriorityQueue;
import java.util.Comparator;

public class PriorityQueueExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // PriorityQueue with natural ordering (min-heap)
        PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();
        minHeap.add(5);
        minHeap.add(1);
        minHeap.add(3);
        System.out.println("Min-Heap: " + minHeap); // Output: Min-Heap: [1, 5, 3]
        System.out.println("Peek: " + minHeap.peek()); // Output: Peek: 1
        System.out.println("Poll: " + minHeap.poll()); // Output: Poll: 1
        System.out.println("Min-Heap after poll: " + minHeap); // Output: Min-Heap after poll: [3, 5]

        // PriorityQueue with custom Comparator (max-heap)
        PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.reverseOrder());
        maxHeap.add(5);
        maxHeap.add(1);
        maxHeap.add(3);
        System.out.println("Max-Heap: " + maxHeap); // Output: Max-Heap: [5, 1, 3]
        System.out.println("Peek: " + maxHeap.peek()); // Output: Peek: 5
        System.out.println("Poll: " + maxHeap.poll()); // Output: Poll: 5
        System.out.println("Max-Heap after poll: " + maxHeap); // Output: Max-Heap after poll: [3, 1]
    }
}"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation of the code:"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`add(element)`: Adds an element to the queue. The queue reorders itself to maintain the priority order."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`peek()`: Retrieves, but does not remove, the head of the queue (the element with the highest priority). Returns `null` if the queue is empty."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`poll()`: Retrieves and removes the head of the queue. Returns `null` if the queue is empty."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`remove(element)`: Removes a single instance of the specified element from this queue, if it is present."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`contains(element)`: Returns true if this queue contains the specified element."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`size()`: Returns the number of elements in the queue."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`isEmpty()`: Returns `true` if the queue is empty, `false` otherwise."},{"order":27,"type":"heading","text":"ArrayDeque"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `ArrayDeque` class implements the Deque (double-ended queue) interface, which extends the Queue interface. It provides the ability to add and remove elements from both ends of the queue. `ArrayDeque` is implemented as a resizable array, making it efficient for adding and removing elements at both ends."},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Characteristics of ArrayDeque"},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Allows adding and removing elements from both ends (Deque)."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Does not allow null elements."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Not thread-safe; use `LinkedBlockingDeque` for concurrent environments."},{"order":33,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Provides O(1) time complexity for adding and removing elements at both ends (amortized)."},{"order":34,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Can be used as a stack (LIFO) or a queue (FIFO)."},{"order":35,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating an ArrayDeque"},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can create an `ArrayDeque` as follows:"},{"order":37,"type":"code","text":"ArrayDeque<Integer> deque = new ArrayDeque<>();"},{"order":38,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Operations with ArrayDeque"},{"order":39,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here are some common operations performed on an `ArrayDeque`:"},{"order":40,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.ArrayDeque;

public class ArrayDequeExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayDeque<Integer> deque = new ArrayDeque<>();

        // Adding elements
        deque.addFirst(1); // Add to the front
        deque.addLast(2);  // Add to the end
        deque.offerFirst(0); // Add to the front (returns false if deque is full)
        deque.offerLast(3);  // Add to the end (returns false if deque is full)

        System.out.println("Deque: " + deque); // Output: Deque: [0, 1, 2, 3]

        // Removing elements
        System.out.println("Remove First: " + deque.removeFirst()); // Output: Remove First: 0
        System.out.println("Remove Last: " + deque.removeLast());   // Output: Remove Last: 3
        System.out.println("Deque after removals: " + deque); // Output: Deque after removals: [1, 2]

        // Peeking elements
        System.out.println("Peek First: " + deque.peekFirst()); // Output: Peek First: 1
        System.out.println("Peek Last: " + deque.peekLast());   // Output: Peek Last: 2

        // Using as a Stack (LIFO)
        ArrayDeque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push(1); // Add to the top
        stack.push(2);
        System.out.println("Stack: " + stack); // Output: Stack: [2, 1]
        System.out.println("Pop: " + stack.pop()); // Output: Pop: 2
        System.out.println("Stack after pop: " + stack); // Output: Stack after pop: [1]
    }
}"},{"order":41,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation of the code:"},{"order":42,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`addFirst(element)`: Adds an element to the front of the deque."},{"order":43,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`addLast(element)`: Adds an element to the end of the deque."},{"order":44,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`offerFirst(element)`: Adds an element to the front of the deque (returns `false` if the deque is full)."},{"order":45,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`offerLast(element)`: Adds an element to the end of the deque (returns `false` if the deque is full)."},{"order":46,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`removeFirst()`: Removes and returns the first element of the deque."},{"order":47,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`removeLast()`: Removes and returns the last element of the deque."},{"order":48,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`peekFirst()`: Retrieves, but does not remove, the first element of the deque."},{"order":49,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`peekLast()`: Retrieves, but does not remove, the last element of the deque."},{"order":50,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`push(element)`: Adds an element to the top of the stack (same as `addFirst`)."},{"order":51,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`pop()`: Removes and returns the top element of the stack (same as `removeFirst`)."},{"order":52,"type":"heading","text":"Choosing Between PriorityQueue and ArrayDeque"},{"order":53,"type":"paragraph","text":"The choice between `PriorityQueue` and `ArrayDeque` depends on the specific requirements of your application:"},{"order":54,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use `PriorityQueue` when you need elements to be processed based on their priority (e.g., task scheduling, event handling)."},{"order":55,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use `ArrayDeque` when you need a double-ended queue (Deque) and require efficient addition and removal of elements from both ends (e.g., implementing a stack or a queue)."},{"order":56,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":57,"type":"paragraph","text":"`PriorityQueue` and `ArrayDeque` are powerful implementations of the Queue interface in Java. `PriorityQueue` provides a way to process elements based on priority, while `ArrayDeque` offers flexibility as a double-ended queue. Understanding their characteristics and use cases allows you to choose the appropriate data structure for your specific needs, leading to more efficient and maintainable code."}]
